\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{BOS diagnostic.}{17}{section*.56}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Every continued-pretraining run (one run per arm). Main runs: 1\,GB of Nepali and 1\,GB of English. Warm-up runs: the same, after training only the embedding matrix on the first 10\% of the stream (Tokens excludes that stage). Held-out BPB is scored in pieces of about 2{,}000 bytes, after the registered prefix (reg.: beginning-of-sequence token for Llama) or the training separator (sep.: end-of-text token); for Qwen the two coincide. The base Llama model was never trained without its beginning-of-sequence token, so its separator score is not meaningful (n/a). All columns other than sep.\ use the registered prefix; for the Llama warm-up runs they are dominated by the scoring artifact of \S  \ref  {sec:cpt} and should not be compared with the main runs. Belebele: zero-shot accuracy on 900 items (chance 0.25); every value lies between 0.25 and 0.29 with 95\% intervals of about $\pm $0.03, so it cannot separate the arms. chrF++: 5-shot FLORES-200 devtest translation, single greedy pass, no intervals. Gen.\ B/s: UTF-8 bytes of Nepali generated per second, measured on GPUs shared with concurrent training runs; it is noisy (the base and \textsc  {R0}{} Qwen models share tokenizer and architecture yet differ by 41\%) and we do not draw conclusions from it.\relax }}{18}{table.caption.55}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cpt}{{8}{18}{Every continued-pretraining run (one run per arm). Main runs: 1\,GB of Nepali and 1\,GB of English. Warm-up runs: the same, after training only the embedding matrix on the first 10\% of the stream (Tokens excludes that stage). Held-out BPB is scored in pieces of about 2{,}000 bytes, after the registered prefix (reg.: beginning-of-sequence token for Llama) or the training separator (sep.: end-of-text token); for Qwen the two coincide. The base Llama model was never trained without its beginning-of-sequence token, so its separator score is not meaningful (n/a). All columns other than sep.\ use the registered prefix; for the Llama warm-up runs they are dominated by the scoring artifact of \S \ref {sec:cpt} and should not be compared with the main runs. Belebele: zero-shot accuracy on 900 items (chance 0.25); every value lies between 0.25 and 0.29 with 95\% intervals of about $\pm $0.03, so it cannot separate the arms. chrF++: 5-shot FLORES-200 devtest translation, single greedy pass, no intervals. Gen.\ B/s: UTF-8 bytes of Nepali generated per second, measured on GPUs shared with concurrent training runs; it is noisy (the base and \RO {} Qwen models share tokenizer and architecture yet differ by 41\%) and we do not draw conclusions from it.\relax }{table.caption.55}{}}
